<problem>

<setup>
  <selectFromSequence name="a" from="2" to="18" />
  <selectFromSequence name="bb" step=" 0.05" from="1.2" to="1.6" />
  <select name="q">
    <option>
      <number>0</number>
      <text>doubling time</text>
      <math>2</math>
    </option>
    <option>
      <number>1</number>
      <text>half-life</text>
      <math>1/2</math>
    </option>
  </select>
  
  <math name="b" simplify="full">$bb-$q[1][1]</math>
  <math name="t_half_double" simplify="full">log($q[1][3])/log($b)</math>
</setup>

Consider the function <m>f(t)=$a($b)^t. \;\;</m> Find the 
$q[1][2], i.e. find <m>t</m> such that <m>f(t)=
$q[1][3] f(0).</m>

<p>
$q[1][2] =
<mathInput name="input1" />
</p>
<p>(If you round your answer, include 5 or more significant digits to be sure to earn full credit.)</p>

<answer>
<award referencesAreResponses="$input1" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.0001"><when>
  $input1 = $t_half_double
</when></award>
<award credit="0.5" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.01" feedbackcodes="numericalerror"><when>
  $input1 = $t_half_double
</when></award>
<award credit="0.25" allowedErrorInNumbers="0.1" feedbackcodes="numericalerror"><when>
  $input1 = $t_half_double
</when></award>
</answer>
$_answer1.feedbacks

<solution>
<p>To find the 
$q[1][2], we need to solve for the time when <m>f(t)=
$q[1][3] f(0).</m>  We do this by dividing the equation by <m>$a</m> (so that the left hand side becomes just an exponential), then taking the logarithm of both sides of the equation.  Note that <m>f(0) = $a($b)^0 = $a</m>. </p>
<md>
  <mrow>f(t)\amp = $q[1][3] f(0)</mrow>
  <mrow>$a($b)^t\amp = $q[1][3] ($a)</mrow>
  <mrow>($b)^t\amp = $q[1][3]</mrow>
  <mrow>\log  ($b)^t\amp = \log \left($q[1][3] \right)</mrow>
  <mrow>t \log  ($b)\amp = \log \left($q[1][3] \right)</mrow>
  <mrow>t\amp = \frac{\log($q[1][3] )}{\log($b)} \approx <round numdigits="5">$t_half_double</round></mrow>
</md>

<p>(It doesn't matter what base logarithm you use; you'll get the same answer for any base.)</p>

<p>Note: you don't need to use a calculator and round you answer.  You can just enter your answer as
<c>log(<text extend="$q[1][3].text" />)/log(<text extend="$b.text" />)</c>
if you prefer.</p>
</solution>

</problem>